#
# Where this node's own updates come from.
#
# Nothing used to ship this line. It was typed by hand on every box, and on
# 2026-08-14 that showed: the controller still carried the release-asset URL
# from before the Pages feed existed - the one that mangles the tilde in a
# version and answers 404 - so `apk add --upgrade` there returned RC=0, said
# "OK", and changed nothing. A node has no business guessing where its own
# software lives, and a silent no-op is the worst way to find out it does not
# know.
#
# Written every time rather than only when absent: a wrong address is exactly
# the case that needs fixing, and this file has no user-owned content to
# preserve. The bootstrap `apk add` still needs an address from somewhere -
# the image, or --repository on the command line - because a package cannot
# deliver the way to reach itself. What this fixes is every update after it.
FEED=https://woziwrt.github.io/bpi-r4-deploy/easymesh/packages.adb
LIST=/etc/apk/repositories.d/easymesh.list

# None of the above applies to a production image, and there this line is not
# merely useless but harmful.
#
# The two models are opposites on purpose. A lab image ships nothing and
# installs the whole stack from this feed, so a node is exactly what its
# package list says and measurements between boards are comparable. A
# production image has the stack baked in and gets its updates as a new image
# through sysupgrade; it has no reason to ever run apk.
#
# Leave the feed on such a node and one `apk upgrade` installs the lab build of
# the same packages on top of the baked one. Which copy the node then runs
# depends on when it was last flashed and when it was last upgraded - the exact
# mixture that meant no two boards in the lab were alike until 2026-08-09, and
# the reason the mesh was taken out of the lab image in the first place. The
# same command has already replayed firstboot once and taken the controller's
# role with it.
#
# Measured 2026-08-17: the first production image did carry this line, written
# right here at first boot. The marker is baked by the production builder,
# because only whoever assembles the image knows which model it is - the node
# cannot tell a package it was born with from one installed a minute later.
if [ "$(cat /etc/easymesh-model 2>/dev/null)" = "production" ]; then
	rm -f "$LIST"
	exit 0
fi

mkdir -p /etc/apk/repositories.d
[ "$(cat "$LIST" 2>/dev/null)" = "$FEED" ] && exit 0

echo "$FEED" > "$LIST"
logger -t easymesh-config "apk feed set to $FEED"
exit 0
